# Action View's namespace — issue #393.
#
# `ActionView::Base` is the `self_type:` of every ERB template unit the plugin compiles. It is declared
# here for one reason: so that the NAME resolves. `Analysis::TemplateUnits#seed` binds an unresolvable
# `self_type:` to `Dynamic[top]`, which is silent but also tells the engine nothing, and a declared
# receiver is what lets a later slice put a real helper surface behind it.
#
# ## Why it enumerates nothing
#
# The same argument `action_controller.rbs` makes about `ActionController::Parameters`, one step stronger.
# A template's implicit-self calls are `link_to`, `form_with`, `t`, `l`, `cache`, `content_for`,
# `image_tag`, `turbo_frame_tag`, every `*_path` / `*_url` route helper, every method in the project's own
# `ApplicationHelper` and `<Controller>Helper`, every `helper_method` a controller exported, and whatever
# the project's gems mixed in. No signature completes that surface, and a declared class DROPS every
# member its signature omits — so enumerating the ten helpers the design note names would turn the other
# thousand into `call.undefined-method` on working templates.
#
# So the class is named and left empty, and `open_receivers:` in the manifest (ADR-26) keeps its whole
# method surface lenient. That is the "lenient / open receivers, never closed" posture stated as code.
#
# `ActionView::UnknownLocal` is deliberately ABSENT — see `ViewUnits::UNKNOWN_LOCAL`. It is the name a
# strict-locals parameter is seeded with precisely because nothing resolves it, so the engine binds
# `Dynamic[top]` while the local's NAME still reaches the parse.

# ## The one declared member, and why it is not a helper
#
# `__rigor_yield` is issue #1047's reading of a LAYOUT's `yield`. `<%= yield %>` is legal ERB and illegal
# Ruby outside a method body, so `ErbCompiler` rewrites the keyword into a call on this receiver before
# either compiler runs — the template-unit seam does not wrap a unit's body in a synthesised method, so the
# body has to parse as written. The name is not Rails', is not reachable from any template a user writes,
# and exists only so the rewritten body resolves.
#
# It returns `String` and nothing narrower. Rails' `yield` hands back whatever the inner template's output
# buffer holds and `yield :sidebar` hands back a `content_for` buffer (an empty `SafeBuffer`, never nil,
# when nothing was provided); a lenient `String` is the
# widest honest reading of that, and it is what keeps a layout from seeding a type the analyzer could not
# justify. `*untyped` covers `yield`, `yield :sidebar` and `yield(:sidebar)` alike.

module ActionView
  class Base
    def __rigor_yield: (*untyped) -> String
  end
end
